── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #1302  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: mseccfg was ambiguously 'optional' — it exists iff a field-defining extension (Zkr/Smmpm/Zicfilp/Smepmp) is present; software misdetection leaves M-mode security enforcement unconfigured
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:579-580  riscv_isa_ext[] (data table)
      smmpm (which defines mseccfg.PMM bits in M-mode) is registered as __RISCV_ISA_EXT_DATA with no superset link to any mseccfg-existence gate. In contrast, smnpm/ssnpm correctly declare riscv_xlinuxenvcfg_exts as their subset. This means the extension that defines mseccfg fields has no mechanism to trigger mseccfg CSR accessibility probing. The kernel never learns whether CSR_MSECCFG (0x747) actually exists.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:428-436  (comment block, line 428-436)
      This comment block explicitly documents the correct CSR-existence pattern: 'the existence of the CSRs is implied by any extension which specifies [ms]envcfg bit(s)' and defines riscv_xlinuxenvcfg_exts[] to auto-enable XLINUXENVCFG whenever any field-defining extension is present. No analogous riscv_xlinuxmseccfg_exts[] exists anywhere in the tree. The comment itself is the architectural proof that the kernel developers understood this pattern was needed but never applied it to mseccfg.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:506-507  riscv_isa_ext[] (data table)
      Zicfilp (which adds the LPE bit to mseccfg) is declared as SUPERSET_VALIDATE of riscv_xlinuxenvcfg_exts — the envcfg existence gate. Zicfilp's LPE bit lives in mseccfg, not envcfg. Routing Zicfilp through the envcfg gate means: (a) XLINUXENVCFG is auto-enabled, making envcfg CSR access safe, but (b) no equivalent gate exists for mseccfg, so the kernel never validates that CSR_MSECCFG is accessible before relying on Zicfilp's LPE functionality.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:201-204  flush_thread
      flush_thread writes envcfg.PMM gated on RISCV_ISA_EXT_SUPM (which is SMNPM in M-mode). SMNPM defines PMM bits in both envcfg and mseccfg, but only envcfg is written here. In M-mode kernels (CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE), the M-mode PMM configuration lives in mseccfg.PMM — this write to envcfg.PMM only configures S/HS-mode pointer masking, leaving mseccfg.PMM uninitialized. The implicit assumption is that M-mode firmware or hardware reset has already set mseccfg.PMM correctly, but this is never verified.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:423-441  tagged_addr_init
      tagged_addr_init probes envcfg.PMM WARL values to detect supported PMLEN settings. In M-mode, the PMM config lives in mseccfg, but this code path exclusively uses CSR_ENVCFG. mseccfg existence is never independently verified before PMM setup. If the platform implements PMM in mseccfg but not envcfg (or with different WARL behaviour), the detected PMLEN values will be wrong and pointer-masking may be silently misconfigured.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:245-249  (macro definitions)
      MSECCFG_PMM bit macros are aliased directly to ENVCFG_PMM. This implies awareness that mseccfg shares the same bit layout as envcfg for PMM, but no code path in arch/riscv/kernel/ or arch/riscv/kvm/ ever uses these macros with csr_write(CSR_MSECCFG, ...). The aliasing is a latent correctness assumption — it only holds if envcfg.PMM and mseccfg.PMM are truly WARL-identical on all implementations, which is not architecturally guaranteed.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:429-430  (macro definitions)
      CSR_MSECCFG (0x747) and CSR_MSECCFGH (0x757) are defined in the header but are dead definitions — a grep across arch/riscv/kernel/ and arch/riscv/kvm/ confirms zero csr_read/csr_write references to either constant. This is the most direct code evidence: the kernel has the CSR address constants but never accesses the register. Any security feature gated on mseccfg bits (PMM in M-mode via Smmpm, SSEED via Zkr, LPE via Zicfilp, PMP enforcement via Smepmp) will be silently non-functional.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:96-97  (macro definitions)
      SMMPM (87) and SMNPM (88) are defined as extension IDs. SMNPM aliases to SUPM (line 123) which gates envcfg.PMM in flush_thread and tagged_addr_init. However, SMNPM also adds PMM bits to mseccfg (the M-mode counterpart), and SMMPM adds PMM bits exclusively to mseccfg. Neither triggers any mseccfg CSR existence check. The linkage is correct for envcfg but the mseccfg half is silently dropped.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:222-228  kvm_riscv_vcpu_isa_enable_allowed
      Zkr is in the KVM ISA extension allow-list. Zkr adds the SSEED bit to mseccfg, which controls seed CSR access permission. The KVM allow-list has no mseccfg-existence precondition. If a guest is granted Zkr but the host mseccfg.SSEED is not configured (because the kernel never touches mseccfg), the guest's seed CSR accesses could be blocked by hardware or behave unpredictably. The allow-list assumes Zkr implies mseccfg accessibility, an assumption not validated.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/archrandom.h:57-70  arch_get_random_seed_longs
      arch_get_random_seed_longs checks RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZKR before accessing csr_seed_long(). Zkr adds the SSEED bit to mseccfg, which gates seed CSR access in hardware. The kernel checks the extension flag but never reads or writes mseccfg to ensure SSEED is enabled. If mseccfg.SSEED defaults to 0 at reset, csr_seed_long() will trap or return degenerate values. The implicit invariant is that firmware or hardware reset sets SSEED=1, but this is not architecturally mandated and is never verified.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/cpufeature.h:161-165  cpu_supports_indirect_br_lp_instr
      cpu_supports_indirect_br_lp_instr gates indirect-branch landing-pad support on RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZICFILP. Zicfilp's LPE (Landing Pad Enable) bit lives in mseccfg, but this helper only checks the ISA extension bitmap — it never verifies that mseccfg is accessible or that LPE has been written. If mseccfg is not writable (e.g., Smstateen0 not configured, or CSR absent), CFI landing-pad enforcement is silently non-functional while the kernel believes it is active.

